Fixes and improvements
- CHECKOUT IDEMPOTENCY: spam-clicking Place Order creates one throwaway order per click until the unique index
  rejects it (422); clicks landing between link-order and mark-cart-completed get 409. Confirmed under dev:slow,
  so the "only ms on a local DB" caveat is settled — it is real, just not damaging: one order, one charge, no
  oversell. Deferred; real fix is a lock on the cart (see "investigate locking").
- create-order compensation orphans addresses: order_address is referenced *by* order (order/models/order.ts:25),
  so no cascade reaches it and every rolled-back order leaves up to two rows. cart_address has the same shape.
- display_id is a serial, so every rolled-back order burns an order number. Gaps are visible to shoppers once
  a race discards five in a row. Only fixable by changing how the number is assigned.
- SOFT DELETE CASCADE in BaseRepository: walk drizzle's FK metadata and soft-delete the children that declare
  onDelete:'cascade', so the model stays the source of truth instead of each module service. Needs a
  module-scoped table registry at bootstrap (no FK crosses modules today). Invert order_address/cart_address
  onto the child so they are covered too. restore() needs the same walk, matched on deletedAt.
- `idempotent` flag in core/workflows/types.ts:16 is dead — nothing reads it, no workflow sets it. Delete.
- Stripe idempotencyKey is never populated — stripe-provider.ts:209 reads it off session context, nothing writes it.
  Wire one before Stripe goes live.

- STALE SHIPPING METHOD AND PAYMENT SESSION SURVIVE AN ADDRESS CHANGE (payment-provider gap): changing the
  cart's shipping address leaves the previously chosen shipping method and the payment collection/session on the
  cart, quoted against the address that is gone. Nothing invalidates them.
  - Two ways in. Signing out mid-checkout: useCheckoutForm.signOut resets the form, so the shopper must re-enter
    an address, but the cart keeps the row and the collection. And "Use a different address" at the delivery
    step, which has the same hole with no sign-out involved. The address seam is the real one; sign-out is a
    route into it.
  - WRONG AMOUNT CAN BE AUTHORIZED. store/payment-collections/[id]/payment-sessions/route.ts creates the session
    with `amount: collection.amount` — snapshotted when the collection was created, i.e. while the old shipping
    method was still on the cart. complete-cart.ts `validate-cart-payments` resolves one collection through
    linkService.repo('cartPaymentCollection').findByCartId and checks only that the session status is in
    PROCESSABLE_STATUSES. It never compares the collection amount to the cart's current total.
  - WRONG RATE CAN SHIP. complete-cart.ts `validate-shipping` re-fetches each method's option and asserts
    option.isEnabled, nothing more. A method quoted for the previous address — different country, different
    zone — passes.
  - Compounding: payment-form.tsx calls createPaymentSession on every radio change, and usePaymentSession calls
    createCollection first each time, so repeated selections can link more than one collection to a cart while
    findByCartId reads a single one. Worth confirming whether createPaymentCollectionForCartWorkflow is
    idempotent per cart.
  - Visible symptom now: after sign-out the summary's Shipping line still shows a price (it renders "Enter
    shipping address" only when cart.shippingMethods is empty) while the radio group beside it is blank.
  - No primitive exists to fix it from the client. There is no DELETE for a shipping method or a payment session
    in src/api/store. POST /store/carts/:id/shipping-methods replaces (soft-deletes existing, then adds) but
    cannot clear, and updateCartWorkflow upserts addresses and touches neither methods nor payments.
  - Three shapes, pick one when this is picked up:
    1. Invalidate at the address seam — updateCartWorkflow drops shipping methods and the payment collection when
       the shipping address changes. Covers both routes in, no new endpoints. This is what Medusa does, via
       refreshCartShippingMethodsWorkflow, which re-lists each method's option for the current cart context and
       deletes any that no longer resolves or no longer prices. Leaves a window at sign-out where the cart is
       untouched until the guest types an address.
    2. Clear at the sign-out seam — new store endpoints, called from signOut. No mismatch window, but two
       endpoints that exist only for logout, and the "use a different address" path stays broken.
    3. Stop creating the session eagerly — move collection + session creation out of the payment radio's
       onValueChange back to place-order time, so there is never a stale session to reset. Kills the wrong-amount
       bug at the root and reduces the problem to shipping methods alone. This is how it worked before the
       one-page checkout refactor.
  - Whichever is chosen, complete-cart should also assert the collection amount equals the cart total. That is
    the check that would have caught all of this, and it is missing regardless of which seam gets the fix.

- CART OWNERSHIP IS UNGUARDED: any request holding a cart id can read and mutate that cart, including one
  belonging to a registered customer — so the cart carries their email, phone and full postal address to whoever
  has the id. `validateCartOwnership` used to reject this; it was deleted deliberately, because with it in place
  signing out mid-checkout left a cart id that 403s on every fetch and the shopper lost their basket.
  - This matches Medusa exactly. Its store cart routes carry no `authenticate` at all (only
    POST /store/carts/:id/customer does) and its handlers are typed `MedusaRequest`, not
    `AuthenticatedMedusaRequest`. The cart id is the bearer token, by design — /store/orders *is* authenticated,
    so the asymmetry is a decision, not an oversight. Medusa has no detach/release operation anywhere: no route,
    no workflow, no step, no SDK method, and no code path that ever sets a cart's customer_id to null.
  - Proper fix is to restore the guard and pair it with a detach, so sign-out releases the cart instead of
    orphaning it: DELETE /store/carts/:id/customer, symmetric with the existing POST, authorized by the guard so
    only the owner can release. Null `customerId` *and* `email`, and soft-delete the cart_address rows — a
    sign-out on a shared device must not leave the last shopper's address prefilled in checkout.
  - Null both or it undoes itself, and this one is live today, not future work: updateCartWorkflow already runs
    findOrCreateCustomerStep, whose findRegisteredCustomer does listCustomers({ email, hasAccount: true }) and
    returns the account. update-cart.ts then writes { customerId, email } back onto the cart. So a cart with
    customerId nulled but the email kept re-attaches to the registered customer the moment the signed-out guest
    retypes their own address at the contact field. (The `TODO(guest)` in carts/route.ts is a different gap —
    cart *creation* not resolving guest emails. Medusa behaves the same way on the update path.)
  - The primitive is already sanctioned upstream even though the operation isn't: Medusa's `updateCartsStep`
    types customer_id and email as `string | null`, the column is nullable, and the step snapshots customer_id
    for compensation. A detach workflow is transfer-cart-customer.ts with the customer id swapped for null.
  - Take the customer id from the auth context only, never the body — that is what Medusa's transfer does.
  - Re-price after detaching only if we ever add customer-group pricing; Medusa runs refreshCartItemsWorkflow
    with force_refresh because its pricing and promotion context include customer.* and customer.groups.*.
  - Worth an ADR when it lands: restoring the guard is a named departure from Medusa, and soft-deleting the
    addresses is a second one (Medusa's transfer leaves addresses and shipping methods untouched).

- INVENTORY IS NOT ACTUALLY RESERVED: placing an order does not reduce what the storefront offers.
  - `createReservationItems` (apps/backend/src/modules/inventory/services/inventory-module-service.ts:173) writes a
    reservation_item row and nothing else. `deleteReservationItems` / `restoreReservationItems` are the same.
    Nothing in the codebase ever writes `inventory_level.reserved_quantity`.
  - `retrieveAvailableQuantity` is `stocked - reserved`, so with reserved permanently 0 availability ignores every
    order that has been placed but not yet fulfilled. Stock only moves at fulfillment, via `adjustInventoryLevel`,
    which touches `stocked_quantity` only.
  - Net effect: the same last unit can be sold an unbounded number of times. The concurrency test in
    src/api/store/carts/__tests__/cart.api.test.ts passes on the `order_cart` unique index, not on inventory —
    it proves one cart completes once, not that stock is respected across different carts.
  - Fix is one decision: keep reserved_quantity as a maintained counter (increment on reserve, decrement on
    release/fulfil, all inside the existing transaction) or drop the column and derive availability by summing
    live reservation_item rows. Either way `confirmInventory` and `buildVariantStock` inherit it for free.

- RESERVATIONS ARE KEYED TO CART LINE ITEMS, BUT READ BY ORDER LINE ITEM.
  - complete-cart.ts:303 writes `lineItemId` from the *cart*'s line items (`cali_…`). cancel-order.ts:40 and
    create-order-fulfillment.ts:138 both call `listReservationItems({ lineItemId })` with *order* line item ids
    (`ordli_…`). They never match.
  - Cancelling an order therefore never releases its stock — the reservation is stranded and the units stay
    unsellable forever (and see above: they were never counted as reserved anyway).
  - Fulfilling an order with managed inventory always throws `No reservation found for managed-inventory item
    ordli_…` from compute-inventory-adjustments.ts, so fulfillment is impossible for any tracked variant.
  - Decide where the mapping lives: re-key the reservation to the order line item in `reserve-inventory` (the
    order already exists by then), or carry a `cartLineItemId` onto order_line_item and look up through it.
  - Blocks the last two files of the workflow-test migration — see docs/backend-test-infrastructure.md.

